How aluminium is made and why it matters
The journey from red rock to the metal we use every day.
Aluminium is everywhere. It&rsquo;s light, strong, and durable, making it the ideal material for things like window frames, vehicle parts, food or drink packaging and power cables .&nbsp; 
But where does aluminium come from and how is it made?
Understanding the journey from red rock to metal helps explain aluminium&rsquo;s versatility and why the way it's produced matters.&nbsp;
Key takeaways:&nbsp; 
Aluminium starts as bauxite, a reddish-brown rock found in the ground. Bauxite is refined into alumina, a fine white powder, which is then smelted into aluminium metal.&nbsp;
Aluminium is widely used because it’s light, strong, corrosion-resistant, conductive, malleable, and recyclable.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Global demand for aluminium is expected to increase by almost 40% by 2030 1 . Producing aluminium is energy-intensive - how it’s made matters.&nbsp;
Rio Tinto is investing in technologies like ELYSIS ® and AP60 while expanding recycling to produce more of our aluminium more efficiently while working to reduce production-related emissions.
How do you make aluminium?
From red rock to white powder &ndash; how we get alumina from bauxite&nbsp;
To make aluminium, we need to start in the ground with bauxite, a naturally-occurring, reddish-brown ore often found in tropical and subtropical regions like Brazil, Guinea and parts of Australia.&nbsp;
Bauxite is known for its reddish hue – a result of the iron oxides present in the ore.
After bauxite is mined, the ore is transported to a refinery where it’s processed to extract a fine white powder called alumina using a multistep industrial process called the Bayer process .
A conveyor &nbsp;at our BC Works operations in Kitimat in Canada moves alumina across the site
From powder to molten metal &ndash; how alumina becomes aluminium&nbsp;
After alumina is refined, it gets transported to a smelter where it's dissolved in a molten bath and an electric current is passed through it, separating aluminium from oxygen.&nbsp;&nbsp;
The result is molten aluminium which is then cast into different forms like ingots, billets or slabs. These can then be rolled, extruded or shaped depending on what customers need.&nbsp;
How is aluminium made?
Before aluminium becomes the shiny metal we all recognise, it starts as bauxite, a reddish-brown ore found in the ground. Bauxite is mined and processed into a fine white powder called alumina which is then used to produce aluminium.
