What is aluminium used for?&nbsp;
Aluminium is useful because it combines several useful properties in one material:&nbsp;
light, but strong
resistant to corrosion
a good conductor of electricity
malleable, which means it can be shaped, rolled, cast and extruded
recyclable , so it can be used again and again.
Often, it does several of these things at once.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And because it&rsquo;s so versatile, aluminium can be mixed and combined with other elements to form specialised alloys or products, depending on what it&rsquo;ll be used for.&nbsp;
That&rsquo;s why aluminium appears in so many parts of everyday life.
Why is aluminium used in so many things?
Aluminium is widely used because it&rsquo;s light, strong, corrosion-resistant, conductive, malleable and recyclable.
It can also be combined with small amounts of other elements to make specialised alloys for different uses.
These properties make aluminium useful in buildings, transport, packaging, electrical systems and renewable energy infrastructure.
Does aluminium rust?
No. Aluminium does not rust in the same way iron or steel can rust.
Rust is a form of iron oxide, and aluminium doesn&rsquo;t contain iron in its pure form.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Instead, when aluminium is exposed to air, it naturally forms a very thin oxide layer on its surface. This layer helps protect the metal from further corrosion which is one reason it's used in buildings, vehicles, and packaging.&nbsp;
Does aluminium conduct electricity?
Yes. Aluminium conducts electricity.
It&rsquo;s not as conductive as copper, but it&rsquo;s lightweight and can be cost-effective in many electrical applications. That combination makes aluminium useful in power cables, transmission lines, electrical components and renewable energy infrastructure.
Because aluminium is lighter than copper, it can be especially useful where weight matters or where long spans of electrical conductor are needed.
What is the melting point for aluminium?
The melting point of aluminium is about 660&deg;C or 1,220&deg;F.
This means aluminium changes from solid to liquid at a much lower temperature than some other metals like steel. This is important in casting, recycling and manufacturing, where aluminium can be melted and reshaped into new products.
Why does the way aluminium is made matter?&nbsp;
Aluminium is already part of everyday life, and demand is expected to keep growing &mdash; by almost 40% globally by 2030 1 &nbsp; &mdash; which makes how it&rsquo;s produced increasingly important.&nbsp;
From bauxite mining to alumina refining, smelting and recycling, each stage of the value chain has environmental considerations.&nbsp;
We&rsquo;re working across several areas to produce more of the aluminium the world needs, responsibly:
1. Energy
How does energy affect aluminium&rsquo;s carbon footprint?&nbsp;
Energy is one of the biggest factors in aluminium&rsquo;s carbon footprint: smelting one tonne of primary aluminium uses about 14 megawatt hours of electricity 2 &ndash; roughly the amount an average Canadian household uses over 15 months 3 .&nbsp;
Our 7 hydropower plants in Quebec &nbsp;and British Columbia &nbsp;generate most of the electricity we use at our aluminium operations in Canada. NZAS &nbsp;and Tasmania&rsquo;s Bell Bay &nbsp;smelter also primarily use hydroelectricity to convert alumina into aluminium.&nbsp;&nbsp;
In Australia, we&rsquo;ve been working on a competitive repowering solution for our aluminium operations for several years to transition away from fossil fuels.
Repowering large industrial assets with renewables, when they were built to run on coal and gas, is complex but we&rsquo;re making good progress. We&rsquo;ve contracted more than 2.8 gigawatts (GW) of new renewable energy and more than 600 megawatts (MW) of storage capacity including:&nbsp;
1.1GW of solar electricity from Upper Calliope Solar Farm, to be built near Gladstone&nbsp;
1.1GW of wind energy from the Bungaban Wind Farm in Queensland&nbsp;
90% of electricity and battery storage capacity from Edify Energy&rsquo;s Smoky Creek and Guthries Gap Solar Power Stations, which includes a 600MW solar farm and a 2,400MWh battery energy storage system&nbsp;
40% of Lightsource bp&rsquo;s Lower Wonga Solar Power Station under construction near Gympie, equivalent to 112MW/3.24MWh of power and associated battery storage.&nbsp;
We also have support from the Queensland and Australian governments to help secure internationally competitive energy to continue aluminium production at Boyne smelter until at least 2040.
Together, these agreements help preserve Queensland&rsquo;s fully integrated aluminium value chain from bauxite mining to smelting and supports thousands of regional jobs and onshore manufacturing.
They also position Boyne smelter to be one of the world&rsquo;s first aluminium smelters underpinned by solar and wind power, making Rio Tinto Australia&rsquo;s largest industrial offtaker of renewable energy.&nbsp;
In New South Wales, Tomago Aluminium &nbsp;has also secured a long-term pathway &nbsp;for its energy supply. A new 10-year power purchase agreement will provide electricity to the smelter through to 2038, with 100% renewable electricity from 2033, supporting the long-term future of Australia&rsquo;s largest aluminium smelter.&nbsp;
2. Research and innovation
What technologies are changing aluminium smelting?
Technology is changing how we produce aluminium: providing the potential for more metal, more efficiently with fewer emissions.&nbsp;
One example is AP60 , is our proprietary aluminium smelting technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;
It&rsquo;s designed to produce more aluminium per cell than conventional smelting technologies, helping improve productivity and efficiency.&nbsp;&nbsp;
What is AP60?
Developed by our research and development teams, the AP60 smelting technology is:
highly productive - each pot produces more than 4.5 tonnes of aluminium per day
space-efficient - AP60 produces more aluminium per square metre needing less physical infrastructure to achieve the same output.
When powered by carbon-free electricity, as it is with hydropower at our Arvida smelter in Quebec, it can produce aluminium with a significantly lower carbon footprint than the global average 4 .&nbsp;&nbsp;
Anodes - large carbon blocks - are essential in the process of turning alumina into aluminium metal. Their job is to help carry the electric current that separates aluminium from oxygen.&nbsp; &nbsp;
In traditional smelting, carbon anodes are &ldquo;consumed&rdquo; as they react with oxygen, creating direct greenhouse gas emissions.
Carbon anode before and after consumption in the aluminium smelting process.
ELYSIS , our joint venture with Alcoa, has developed technology that replaces carbon anodes with inert anodes. The venture is supported by the governments of Canada and Quebec, through Investissement Qu&eacute;bec, as well as Apple.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Compared to the conventional smelting process, using inert anodes eliminates direct greenhouse gas emissions from the smelting process, producing oxygen instead. We&rsquo;re currently testing the commercial-sized cell in an industrial demonstration plant at our smelter in Alma in Quebec.&nbsp;&nbsp;
While technology doesn&rsquo;t remove every challenge in the aluminium value chain, it&rsquo;s an important part of how we&rsquo;re working to produce aluminium more efficiently and with a lower emissions profile.
3. Circularity
Is aluminium recyclable?
Aluminium can be recycled again and again, using up to 95% less energy than producing primary aluminium 5 .&nbsp;
This makes recycled aluminium, also known as secondary aluminium, an important part of meeting rising demand for lower-carbon aluminium while reducing the strain on primary production.
We&rsquo;re helping to bolster aluminium supply with our Matalco joint venture, which produces recycled aluminium products in North America. We&rsquo;ve also developed closed-loop systems with partners in Australia to keep the metal circulating and in use.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Aluminium will remain essential to everyday life and the infrastructure of the future. The challenge and opportunity are to meet growing demand, while continuing to improve how the metal is produced and kept in circulation. 
